prompt: Load the `kdevkit` skill from ~/.claude/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md or ~/.kiro/steering/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md — whichever path exists. Begin your response with the literal line `[kdevkit] applies` on its own line. Then answer in 4–8 sentences: I'm starting a new feature in this kdevkit-aware project. What will you do — from the moment I say "let's start feature X" through the first implementation slice?
expect_substr: [kdevkit] applies
expected_narrative: A correct answer covers four behaviors. (1) Looks up the feature file under specs/feature/ — exact path/case format isn't required, but the answer must show it knows where the spec lives. (2) Checks specs/backlog/ first for a matching scoped item and promotes it if found; only runs the four short interviews (Requirements, Design, Test Strategy, Implementation Plan) when nothing is scoped. The word "promote" or equivalent is enough — the literal command verb is not required. (3) Checks project.md for a worktree-preference signal at feature-start; either suggests a worktree (when project.md prefers) or proceeds branch-only when project.md is silent. This is a parallel decision to the spec lookup, not optional. (4) Phase gating — stops after each phase and waits for explicit instruction before the next; "yolo" mode is the override. Wrong answers: jumping straight to coding; auto-creating a worktree without checking project.md; running all four interviews without checking the backlog; chaining phases automatically.
tools: claude,kiro
